---
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import CodeTabs from "../../components/CodeTabs.astro";
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
const version = "v0.4.0";
const release = `https://github.com/aslilac/squill/releases/download/${version}`;
---

<Docs
	page="install"
	title="Install"
	lede="Four ways to get the binary, in rough order of how little you have to think about it."
>
	<section>
		<h2>mise</h2>
		<p>
			<a href="https://mise.jdx.dev">mise</a> and its GitHub backend pull a
			precompiled binary from the latest release, picking the one that matches
			whatever it is running on. Nothing has to know the machine&rsquo;s os or
			architecture, which is what makes it the easy one to drop into CI.
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="sh"
			code={`mise use -g github:aslilac/squill
squill --check .`}
		/>
		<p>
			Pin a version with <code>github:aslilac/squill@{version}</code>. mise
			verifies GitHub artifact attestations and SLSA provenance on install.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="nix">
		<h2>Nix</h2>
		<p>
			The flake builds squill from source for Linux on x86_64 and arm64 and
			macOS on arm64, as its default package. Run it once, keep it for a shell,
			install it, or make it part of your own flake:
		</p>
		<CodeTabs
			tabs={[
				{
					id: "nix-run",
					label: "nix run",
					lang: "sh",
					code: `nix run github:aslilac/squill -- --check .`,
				},
				{
					id: "nix-shell",
					label: "nix shell",
					lang: "sh",
					code: `nix shell github:aslilac/squill
squill --check .`,
				},
				{
					id: "nix-profile",
					label: "nix profile",
					lang: "sh",
					code: `nix profile install github:aslilac/squill
squill --check .`,
				},
				{
					id: "nix-flake",
					label: "flake input",
					lang: "nix",
					code: `{
	inputs = {
		nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";
		# squill builds with the nixpkgs and Rust toolchain it pins itself,
		# so it needs no \`follows\`.
		squill.url = "github:aslilac/squill";
	};

	outputs = { nixpkgs, squill, ... }:
		let
			system = "x86_64-linux";
			pkgs = nixpkgs.legacyPackages.\${system};
		in
		{
			devShells.\${system}.default = pkgs.mkShell {
				packages = [ squill.packages.\${system}.default ];
			};
		};
}`,
				},
			]}
		/>
	</section>

	<section id="cargo">
		<h2>Cargo</h2>
		<p>
			Cargo can clone the source, check out the release tag, and install the
			binary in one command. This is the route that needs a Rust toolchain.
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="sh"
			code={`cargo install --git https://github.com/aslilac/squill.git --tag ${version} cli --bin squill`}
		/>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Release archives</h2>
		<p>
			Every tag publishes archives to{" "}
			<a href="https://github.com/aslilac/squill/releases">GitHub releases</a>:
			Linux and Windows on x86_64 and arm64, and macOS on arm64. The tab for the
			machine you&rsquo;re reading this on opens first.
		</p>
		<CodeTabs
			tabs={[
				...[
					["x86_64", "x86_64"],
					["arm64", "aarch64"],
				].map(([arch, target]) => ({
					id: `linux-${arch}`,
					label: `Linux ${arch}`,
					os: "linux",
					arch,
					lang: "sh",
					code: `curl -fsSL ${release}/squill-${version}-${target}-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz \\
	| sudo tar xz -C /usr/local/bin/
squill --version`,
				})),
				{
					id: "macos-arm64",
					label: "macOS arm64",
					os: "macos",
					arch: "arm64",
					lang: "sh",
					code: `sudo mkdir -p /usr/local/bin
curl -fsSL ${release}/squill-${version}-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz \\
	| sudo tar xz -C /usr/local/bin/
squill --version`,
				},
				...[
					["x86_64", "x86_64"],
					["arm64", "aarch64"],
				].map(([arch, target]) => ({
					id: `windows-${arch}`,
					label: `Windows ${arch}`,
					os: "windows",
					arch,
					lang: "powershell",
					code: `$url = "${release}/squill-${version}-${target}-pc-windows-msvc.zip"
$dir = "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\squill"
Invoke-WebRequest $url -OutFile "$env:TEMP\\squill.zip"
Expand-Archive "$env:TEMP\\squill.zip" -DestinationPath $dir -Force
# Put it on your PATH (new terminals pick it up):
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("Path",
	[Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable("Path", "User") + ";$dir", "User")`,
				})),
			]}
			selected="linux-x86_64"
		/>
		<p>
			There&rsquo;s no build for Intel Macs;{" "}
			<a href="docs/install/#cargo">Cargo</a> builds one from source.
		</p>
	</section>
</Docs>
